Introduction
Part 4
of this series extracted the divergence logic into
DivergenceEngine.mqh
and demonstrated it in a small Adaptive Parabolic SAR EA. That article closed by pointing at something the demonstration had deliberately left alone. In most systems, Parabolic SAR is a trailing-stop tool rather than an entry trigger, and the same adaptive acceleration factor can be used to tighten or loosen a trailing stop on an already-open position from an Expert Advisor that knows nothing about this series. This part builds that.
AdaptivePSARTrail.mqh
is a header that an existing Expert Advisor can include to gain an adaptive Parabolic SAR trailing stop, and it can trail positions that it did not open.
Moving the stop to the current SAR level as each bar closes looks like the whole job. Two things make it more than that. Parabolic SAR changes sides. Its dots sit below price during an uptrend and above price during a downtrend, and the moment price crosses them, they jump to the opposite side. A position does not change sides with them, so at every flip the indicator briefly produces a level that cannot legally serve as a stop for the position it is meant to protect. A broker can also refuse stops that are arithmetically correct. There is a minimum distance from the price at which a stop may be placed, a separate distance within which an existing stop cannot be modified, and a tick grid on which the level must sit. The utility adds a rule of its own on top: a trailing stop never moves backwards. If these checks are treated as a single condition, the stop may stop moving with no clear reason.
AdaptivePSARTrail.mqh
owns its own SAR series and its own
DivergenceEngine
instance, so the host Expert Advisor adds four calls and manages nothing else. The acceleration factor responds to an opposing divergence in whichever direction the trader chooses, and a position is qualified by symbol and magic number rather than by the code that opened it. The companion file,
DEMO_PSAR_Trailing.mq5
, runs the utility in two modes: attached to its own test trades so you can watch the behavior in the Strategy Tester, or attached to nothing of its own, trailing another Expert Advisor's positions.
Scope of This Article
In this article, we will:
Explain why this part ships a header rather than another demonstration EA.
Design the configuration struct and the four calls that form the integration contract.
Adapt the acceleration factor from the divergence state, one bar at a time.
Handle the side change that makes a naive SAR trailing stop unsafe.
Order the broker and improvement checks, and name the result each one returns.
Compile the trading half out for indicators.
Build the demonstration EA in both modes and set out the runs that verify it.
Why a Reusable Header
One chart runs one Expert Advisor, so trailing logic shipped as an EA has two ways in: replace the system it was meant to improve, or run on a second chart and reach across to the first one's positions by symbol and magic number. The second route works, and the demonstration EA in this article does exactly that. It also needs a chart, a second program in the terminal, and a magic number that stays in step with the system it is trailing. A header removes all three for anyone who can recompile the host, and leaves the second-chart route available for the cases where they cannot.
Three design decisions follow. The utility owns its own state. One instance holds one symbol, one timeframe, one SAR series, and one
DivergenceEngine
instance, so the host never has to size a buffer or follow the engine's integration sequence itself. Ownership of a position is decided by symbol and magic number rather than by which code opened it. An instance configured with another Expert Advisor's magic numbers will trail that Expert Advisor's positions, with no shared code and no modification to it. The trading methods are optional. An indicator cannot send trade requests, so those methods and their
CTrade
dependency are compiled out behind a single define.
What the Previous Part Already Settled
Three points carry over into this article and are not repeated here.
MQL5's built-in
iSAR()
fixes its acceleration factor when the handle is created, which is why the Parabolic SAR is calculated directly rather than read from a handle.
The divergence engine stays neutral about what a divergence means, reporting type and age and leaving the interpretation to its consumer. For a trailing stop, that neutrality has a concrete meaning that the entry case did not: reducing the acceleration factor keeps the stop farther from price for longer and gives the trend more room, while increasing it draws the stop in faster and can lock in profit sooner.
The divergence engine knows nothing about what consumes it. This header sits on the consumer side of that boundary and extends it by one step: the engine still knows nothing about Parabolic SAR and now knows nothing about positions either.
The header drives the engine through the same setup, refresh, per-bar, and teardown sequence documented in the
previous part
, and nothing in the engine changed for this one.
Designing the Utility
The Configuration Struct
The header is configured through a single struct that the host fills in once:
