Four things are deliberate. A symbol missing from Market Watch can report zero for its properties, so it is selected and its point size checked before anything is divided by it. The lookback is raised rather than rejected when it falls below what the engine needs, because a host that requests too little history is better served by a working window than by a failed startup. The
CTrade
settings sit behind the compile guard since an indicator build has no
CTrade
to configure. A first refresh also runs immediately, so the opening tick usually has a level to offer already. If history is not available yet, initialization still succeeds. The log reports that the refresh found no data, and the ready flag remains false until a refresh completes. Until then, queries return no data and the trailing pass does nothing. The next call to
Refresh()
acts as the retry. A failure is logged rather than treated as fatal because history is frequently incomplete at startup.
The refresh itself is short because the work lives in the engine and in the SAR loop:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Recompute the oscillator, divergence state and SAR series        |
//| Call once per new bar.                                           |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CAdaptivePSARTrail::Refresh(
void
)
  {
   m_ready =
false
;
if
(!CopyPrices())
return
false
;
if
(!UpdateDivergenceRSI(m_engine, m_limit))
return
false
;

   InitDivergenceBoundaries(m_engine, m_limit);
   ComputeSeries();

   m_lastRefreshBar =
iTime
(m_symbol, m_tf,
0
);
   m_ready =
true
;
return
true
;
  }
The ready flag is cleared first and set only on success, so a failed refresh leaves every query returning nothing rather than returning data from a partially filled buffer. The refresh and the trailing attempt are separate calls because they answer to different clocks. Recalculating the series is the expensive work, and none of it changes inside a bar. Whether the broker will accept a modification depends on the live price, so the attempt runs as often as the host calls it.
UpdateDivergenceRSI()
is named for the one source that needs it. With RSI selected, it copies the RSI indicator's buffer into the engine before the loop starts. With MPO selected, it returns at once, because the MPO oscillator is calculated bar by bar inside the series walk.
The Calculation and Trading Halves
Of the twenty public methods, eighteen perform no trading call. Only the trailing pass and the per-ticket method send anything to the server. The rest either read what the last refresh stored or, in the case of the proposed stop, read the live tick and the broker's two distance properties, and none of them changes anything on the account. Maintaining that division is what allows the trading half to be removed for indicators without disturbing the rest.
Adapting the Acceleration Factor
Parabolic SAR has one sensitivity control. The acceleration factor begins at the base step, and each time the price sets a new extreme in the direction of the trend, the step is added to it, up to a configured ceiling. A larger factor makes the SAR advance toward the price more quickly. Adaptation changes the size of the step contributed by a particular bar:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Step for one bar, adapted only by a divergence that argues       |
//| against the direction the SAR is currently trailing              |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CAdaptivePSARTrail::AdaptiveStepAt(
const
int
shift,
const
int
trendDir)
  {
double
stepNow = m_cfg.SARStep;
if
(!m_cfg.EnableAdaptive)
return
stepNow;
int
divType   = GetLastDivergenceType(m_engine, shift);
int
barsSince = GetBarsSinceDivergence(m_engine, shift);
if
(divType ==
0
|| barsSince > m_cfg.DivBarsLimit)
return
stepNow;
//--- A bullish divergence during a downtrend, or a bearish one during an uptrend
bool
opposes = (trendDir ==
1
&& divType == -
1
) || (trendDir == -
1
&& divType ==
1
);
if
(!opposes)
return
stepNow;
if
(m_cfg.AdaptMode == PSAR_ADAPT_REDUCE)
      stepNow = m_cfg.SARStep * (
1.0
- m_cfg.AdaptFactor);
else
stepNow = m_cfg.SARStep * (
1.0
+ m_cfg.AdaptFactor);
//--- Confine to a usable range
if
(stepNow <
0.0001
)
      stepNow =
0.0001
;
if
(stepNow > m_cfg.SARMaximum)
      stepNow = m_cfg.SARMaximum;
return
stepNow;
  }
Four conditions must be met before the step changes. Adaptation has to be switched on. With it off the base step is returned on every bar, and what trails the position is a plain Parabolic SAR, computed by this header from its own seed rather than read from a handle, with the differences that follow from that set out in the limitations. A divergence has to exist, since the engine reports zero when there is none. It must satisfy
DivBarsLimit
, because a divergence from two hundred bars ago is not necessarily evidence about the current bar, and without that limit a single signal could leave the adaptation active indefinitely. It must also oppose the direction the SAR is currently following, since a regular divergence that agrees with the trend does not call for a change in what the SAR is already doing.
AdaptFactor
is a fraction of the base step, so a factor of 0.3 in reduce mode multiplies the step by 0.7 on that bar. The two confines keep the result within a usable range. A step of zero would freeze the acceleration factor at whatever it had reached, and a step larger than the ceiling achieves nothing a step equal to it would not.
The step is calculated per bar rather than once per series because the SAR computation walks from the oldest processed bar forward and requests a step for each bar. The acceleration factor built up across a trend is therefore the result of what each bar was permitted to add. That is why the SAR is computed here rather than read from a handle: a handle's step is fixed when the handle is created, and a new handle computes its whole history with one step, while this needs one bar's step to differ without rewriting the bars before it.
The Series Walk
The loop that requests each bar's step is also where the trend flips and where ResetOnTrendChange takes effect:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| The Parabolic SAR itself, walked from the oldest processed bar   |
//| forward to the current one so each bar's step can differ         |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CAdaptivePSARTrail::ComputeSeries(
void
)
  {
int
limit = m_limit;
//--- Seed the oldest bar. Direction of that single candle is an arbitrary
//--- starting guess; the SAR converges away from it within a few flips.
int
trend = (m_close[limit] > m_open[limit]) ?
1
: -
1
;
double
sar   = (trend ==
1
) ? m_low[limit]  : m_high[limit];
double
ep    = (trend ==
1
) ? m_high[limit] : m_low[limit];
double
af    = m_cfg.SARStep;
for
(
int
i = limit; i >=
0
; i--)
     {
//--- Divergence state for this bar. The engine owns it entirely.
CalculateOscillator(m_engine, i, m_open, m_close, m_point);
      DetectDivergence(m_engine, i, m_high, m_low, limit);
//--- Trend flip: the SAR jumps to the extreme point of the trend
//--- that just ended, and the acceleration factor starts over
bool
flipped =
false
;
if
(trend ==
1
&& m_low[i] < sar)
        {
         trend   = -
1
;
         sar     = ep;
         ep      = m_low[i];
         af      = m_cfg.SARStep;
         flipped =
true
;
        }
else
if
(trend == -
1
&& m_high[i] > sar)
           {
            trend   =
1
;
            sar     = ep;
            ep      = m_high[i];
            af      = m_cfg.SARStep;
            flipped =
true
;
           }
if
(flipped && m_cfg.ResetOnTrendChange)
         ResetDivergenceState(m_engine, i);
//--- The step this bar is allowed to add to the acceleration factor
double
stepNow = AdaptiveStepAt(i, trend);
//--- A new extreme point is what earns an acceleration increase
if
(trend ==
1
)
        {
if
(m_high[i] > ep)
           {
            ep = m_high[i];
            af =
MathMin
(af + stepNow, m_cfg.SARMaximum);
           }
        }
else
{
if
(m_low[i] < ep)
           {
            ep = m_low[i];
            af =
MathMin
(af + stepNow, m_cfg.SARMaximum);
           }
        }
//--- Store what this bar actually used
m_sar[i]   = sar;
      m_trend[i] = trend;
      m_step[i]  = stepNow;
//--- Project the SAR for the next (more recent) bar
sar = sar + af * (ep - sar);
if
(trend ==
1
)
         sar =
MathMin
(sar,
MathMin
(m_low[i], (i < limit) ? m_low[i+
1
]  : m_low[i]));
else
sar =
MathMax
(sar,
MathMax
(m_high[i], (i < limit) ? m_high[i+
1
] : m_high[i]));
     }
  }
Each pass through the loop does the same things in the same order. The engine first updates its divergence state for the bar. The flip test runs next, and on a flip the SAR jumps to the extreme point of the trend that just ended and the acceleration factor starts over. With
ResetOnTrendChange
set, the engine's divergence state is cleared on that same bar, before the step is requested, so a divergence from the trend that just ended cannot adapt the first bar of the new one. Only then is the bar's step requested, and it is added to the acceleration factor only if the bar sets a new extreme point.
Last, the bar's values are stored and the SAR is projected for the next bar, kept at or below the last two lows in an uptrend and at or above the last two highs in a downtrend. Because a step is consumed only on a bar that sets a new extreme, the reported step is the step in effect rather than the step consumed, and the adapted flag marks bars where an adaptation was active rather than bars where it changed the value.
ResetOnTrendChange
defaults to
false
, which preserves divergence history across flips, but a trader who treats each SAR trend as an independent episode will want it enabled. The oldest bar's direction is taken from that single candle, a starting guess the series moves away from within a few flips; the
limitations
come back to it.
Nothing in the walk reads a bar newer than the one it is computing. For
bar i
,
CalculateOscillator()
reads
bar i
and older bars only, whichever source is selected.
DetectDivergence()
then tests whether
bar i
+
PivotLength
is an oscillator pivot by comparing it with the
PivotLength
bars on each side, and the newest of those is
bar i
itself. A divergence is therefore recorded on the bar that confirms the pivot,
PivotLength
bars after it, and never on the pivot bar. With the default
PivotLength
of 2, a pivot is known two closed bars after it formed.
The trailing pass proposes
StopPrice(1)
, the SAR of the last closed bar. Bar 1's own high and low decide whether that level flips, and the steps that moved it were taken on bar 2 and older, so every input to it comes from closed bars. Bar 0, which is still forming, is also computed on each refresh from the ticks it has so far, but the level the trailing pass sends never comes from it; only the checks read the live price.
The Side Check
A long position is being trailed by a SAR sitting below price. The price falls through the SAR, and on the following bar, the SAR is above the price. A trailing routine that reads the current SAR value and sends it as the stop has attempted to place a stop-loss above the market on a long position.
Fig. 1. A trailing stop cannot point the wrong way.
The trade server refuses a buy stop-loss above the bid, or a sell stop-loss below the ask, as an invalid stop, and the position keeps the stop it already had. Without a side check, the routine keeps sending the same invalid level while the SAR stays on the wrong side. Each attempt is refused, generating journal spam, while the existing stop remains unchanged. The mistake is also easy to make, because the naive routine is right on most bars. Whenever the SAR is on the correct side it produces the right level, and it only goes wrong at flips, which are the moments a trailing stop matters most.
One check, placed before any other work, prevents it:
//--- The side check. The SAR flips; a position does not
if
(m_cfg.RequireTrendAgreement)
     {
if
(isBuy  && sar >= tick.bid)
return
PSAR_TRAIL_WRONG_SIDE;
if
(!isBuy && sar <= tick.ask)
return
PSAR_TRAIL_WRONG_SIDE;
     }
Comparing Against Bid and Ask
A long position's stop is evaluated against bid, and a short position's against ask, so the check uses those two prices rather than a single mid or close value. Using one price would shift the comparison by the spread, which can determine the outcome in a marginal case. A SAR sitting one point below the close of a long position can still be above the bid, particularly on an instrument with a wide spread.
Trend Agreement as a Setting
With the agreement requirement enabled, which is the default, a flipped SAR produces a wrong-side result, and the position keeps its existing stop until the SAR returns to the correct side. That also means the trailing stop makes no change during a flip. Some traders read a SAR flip against an open position as an exit signal rather than as a temporary absence of a stop level, and the utility does not overrule that reading. The trend direction and the flip flag are both exposed, so a host Expert Advisor can close the position itself if that is its policy.
With the requirement switched off, a flipped SAR is still offered as a candidate. The stops-level check then refuses it, because that check refuses any level at or beyond the bid for a buy, or at or beyond the ask for a sell, before it measures the minimum distance, so the log reports a distance problem rather than a side problem. The same check refuses a candidate that a positive
OffsetPoints
has pushed to or past the price. In both cases no stop at or beyond the price is sent, even when the broker reports a stops level of zero and
MinStopBufferPoints
is zero too.
The Remaining Gates
The proposal runs as a sequence of checks in a fixed order, and the order matters because each stage assumes the previous one has passed. One row in the table is not a check: offset and tick alignment changes the candidate rather than judging it, and it sits where it does so that every check after it measures the level that will actually be sent.
Step
What it does
Result when it refuses
