The two properties answer different questions. SYMBOL_TRADE_STOPS_LEVEL is the minimum distance from price at which a stop may sit, so its check measures the candidate against price and asks whether the new level can be set at all. SYMBOL_TRADE_FREEZE_LEVEL is the distance within which an order already in place is treated as pending execution, so its check measures the existing stop against price and asks whether the level already there can be touched. A trailing stop meets the second condition regularly because creeping toward the price is what it does.
MinStopBufferPoints
exists because some brokers report a stops level of zero while applying dynamic, spread-dependent limits instead. With a reported zero, the check still refuses a level at or beyond the price, and any further minimum the broker applies is left to the server, so the configuration field places the trader's own floor beneath the broker's reported value, with the larger of the two used.
The freeze check is skipped when the broker reports zero and when the position has no stop yet. A position without a stop takes the first candidate that clears the earlier checks, which allows an Expert Advisor to open without a stop and let the utility set the first one. Neither check measures the proposed level against the freeze distance. If a broker applies that distance to the new level as well, the refusal comes from the server, and it arrives as PSAR_TRAIL_MODIFY_FAILED with the server's own return code in the log.
