What the Log Shows
One line per refusal would be too many. The trailing pass runs on every tick, and a position waiting out a SAR flip meets the same refusal on every tick of every bar, which on real ticks is hundreds of identical lines for one decision. The verbose log is therefore throttled to one line per ticket per bar: the first refusal a ticket meets in a bar is printed and the rest are dropped, so the log reads as one entry per position per bar with the reason attached. Successful modifications are not throttled. The candidate is fixed for the whole bar, so once a stop has moved there is nothing left to improve until the next refresh, and a position is modified at most once per bar. A refusal from the server is not throttled either, because it is the one message that should never be missing, even though a server that keeps refusing is then reported on every attempt.
The named results cover the checks the header makes itself. Anything the server decides, whether a dynamic stop limit, a freeze applied to the new level or a closed market, arrives as PSAR_TRAIL_MODIFY_FAILED, and the return code and description printed beside it are the only detail the header can give.
